Rational Owned Choice: Finite Algebras of Multiplayer Games

The paper is overleaf/main.tex; its technical report is overleaf/tr.tex. The
mathematical audit entrypoints are root Paper.lean (90 main-paper and cited
results) and Report.lean (156 further report statements and proof ingredients).
Every theorem repeats its complete proposition and has a single named library
theorem as its proof. The scope is the entire main paper and the report material
its claims need, rather than every result in the technical report.

The paper sources are in the Overleaf project, checked out at overleaf/. It is a
separate git repository and is not tracked here. Their history before the move
is in this repository's log, up to commit 0c98c5c, where they are at the root
(main.tex, tr.tex, references.bib, sections/).

Core term-operation classifications

The current technical report collects the core results in
overleaf/sections/appendix-core.tex and its included appendices. The extended
classifications from the November characterization work are indexed by the
appendix overview in the archived sections/appendix-core-basis.tex at commit
fdc69235aeac90c138b34be1aca271fd525c9dc5 of this repository. In that revision,
sections/appendix-core-guard-reachability.tex contains the two-input
observer-cover classification and three-input recognition test (search for
“two-input cover” and “Three-input observer covers”). Its
sections/appendix-verification.tex records the verification status. Run these
in this repository, not in overleaf/:

    git show fdc6923:sections/appendix-core-basis.tex
    git show fdc6923:sections/appendix-core-guard-reachability.tex

For fixed K >= 3 the current report proves PSPACE-completeness for both the
full operation table and the explicit source-probe table. The proof is in
overleaf/sections/appendix-core-table-recognition.tex. The Lean theorems
Paper.core_explicit_table_reduction and Report.explicit_source_table_reduction
compare the actual tables with truth of an arbitrary quantified CNF input.
KFOL/Core/ExplicitTableReduction.lean also gives the polynomial bounds on the
number of entries. The verification appendix records the machine-proof scope.
The reproducible table, rectangle and offer-history checks from this result
are indexed at checks/open-problem/README.md.

Build and verify

Use Lean through elan; lean-toolchain selects Lean 4.36.0. The dependency
versions are fixed by lakefile.toml and lake-manifest.json. Fetch dependencies
and the mathlib cache, then run the checks:

    lake exe cache get
    make check

make check (also make check-lean) runs lake build Paper Report. Warnings are
errors. Each entrypoint ends with #audit_namespace: every declaration in its
namespace is checked transitively against the standard axioms propext,
Classical.choice and Quot.sound. The theorem statements and their proofs are
versioned in Lean; no JSON inventory is required. The optional whole-source
audit is lake build KFOL.Audit, and the independent algebra import-boundary
audit is lake build KFOL.Audit.Algebra. Lean regressions remain under tests/.

To build the manuscripts with TeX Live and latexmk:

    make paper

This runs the build in overleaf/, which has its own Makefile. The PDFs are
overleaf/build/main.pdf and overleaf/build/tr.pdf. Each document reads the
other's numbering from committed overleaf/sections/labels-main.tex and
overleaf/sections/labels-tr.tex, so either builds alone. make paper refreshes
these snapshots; commit them in the overleaf repository after changes that
renumber results. On Overleaf, select either main.tex or tr.tex as the main
document and compile with pdfLaTeX. make clean removes overleaf/build/; Lake's
compiled artifacts are under .lake/.

Only Lean sources, build tools, dependency pins, license and repository
instructions are tracked here. The .gitignore allowlist keeps local notes,
auxiliary checks and verification exports out of the repository.

Artifact and review entry points

    Paper.lean        91 complete main-paper and cited mathematical statements
    Report.lean       161 further audited report statements and proof ingredients
    KFOL/Definitions/ Mathematical semantics, syntax and word formats
    KFOL/Results/     Library theorem assembly and detailed certificates
    KFOL/Algebra/     Independent mathlib-only algebra
    KFOL/Algorithms/  Word codecs and executable algorithms
    KFOL/Complexity/  Native machine runtime, reductions and class certificates
    KFOL/Strategic/   Full-profile SPE and arbitrary-domain public quantifiers
    KFOL/Rounds/      Sealed-round and sequential-equilibrium semantics
    KFOL/Audit.lean   All-source, transitive axiom audit
    tests/            Lean statement, semantics and machine regressions
    overleaf/         Paper sources, in a separate repository: main.tex and
                      tr.tex (main paper and technical report), sections/
                      (manuscript sections and committed label snapshots)

Read the mathematical vocabulary first in KFOL/Definitions/PaperAlgebra.lean,
PaperGames.lean and Complexity.lean, then follow their underlying definitions.
They are front doors to the semantics, not the whole definition closure.
The previous 140 full statement contracts remain in
KFOL/Results/Certificates.lean under KFOL.Results. Codec, descriptor and machine contracts
belong there and in their implementation modules, while the root entrypoints
state the mathematical results.

GameTheory supports the strategic and rounds branches. Pure algebra and word
definitions do not import it or the machine-complexity libraries; the import
audit checks that boundary. The machine branch uses ComplexityLib's independent multitape Turing
model, with explicit input/output encodings and charged intermediate storage.
GameTheory is pinned to its v4.36.0 release,
c20fa93ca3acdeb05fa75785788ce9a763544bcc, and Cslib to
254e30e6d4299c51323aaeaf5f51a4763b375d4a. ComplexityLib is pinned to
481355bbf30f161a600c5761053f42da888e7b0f in
github.com/elazarg/complexitylib-community: upstream revision
054834a4a4e6ac3ec50ed770bacea7fc42eccc7d with one lemma adapted to mathlib
v4.36.0. Lean and mathlib use 4.36.0.

The earlier Std-only development is retired. Its sources and historical
certificates remain available at commit 7014dbb, for example with
`git show 7014dbb:lean/FullAlgebra.lean`; they are not a second current build or
evidence for unfinished report-only results.

Known source refutation

Ghani--Kupke--Lambert--Nordvall Forsberg's "Compositional Game Theory with
Mixed Strategies" (arXiv:2009.06831v1), Definitions 11--12, has finite
counterexamples to the unit law Id;K = K in Lemma 16 (a right unit in the
source's circle notation) and associativity in Theorem 15. Convex closing does
not repair associativity. Do not use those laws as established mixed-game
composition results.

The self-contained argument is in overleaf/sections/appendix-open-games.tex,
"Finite obstructions to the proposed mixed open-game category". The external
Lean proofs are pinned to GameTheory v1-final, revision
02898a2d8b918f9b106a683420ca78c99867560e:
https://github.com/elazarg/GameTheory/tree/02898a2d8b918f9b106a683420ca78c99867560e/GameTheory/Languages/OpenGame
The archived scripts/check_external_open_game.py and verification/open-game-v1.json
rebuild and audit an isolated checkout at that pin. Do not modify the sibling
GameTheory checkout. This refutes the
specified composition, not probabilistic open games generally. The paper's
closed-round sequential-equilibrium recursion is proved separately.

Auxiliary evidence archive

The earlier independent Python checks, their data, verification inventories and
definition indexes, and their supporting scripts are archived at commit
2099f2560825ab7e1617ecc6ebb6d65fa0f07ecd. Paths to these files in the technical
report refer to that archive. For example:

    git show 2099f25:checks/README.md
    git show 2099f25:verification/audit-guide.md

Existing local copies are ignored and may still be used. They are not required
to build or audit the Lean entrypoints or compile either manuscript.
